{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Node1 runs an enabled host firewall whose required rules persist across restart.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Firewalld was active and enabled with matching runtime and permanent rules. After node1 restarted to a different boot identity, firewalld and sshd recovered, both rules remained present, and the final state was healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-90c9f13310a7487c","cmd-b9ee321613f44653","cmd-e748cbccdb724242","cmd-1fd4808a5b4446ec","cmd-49e8500c0f934aef","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"SSH to node1 is allowed from any source address.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The active public zone on node1 exposes the SSH service without a source restriction. Direct TCP/22 checks succeeded from both node2 and node3 before and after node1's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-87288c2d0d7149f1","cmd-99966b48c8c64a69","cmd-05efaddb4e9d4d62","cmd-c0ff7ff50ef0446f","cmd-a873c15997154fb7","cmd-49e8500c0f934aef"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"TCP port 9000 on node1 is allowed from node2 only.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The runtime and permanent configurations contain a higher-priority accept rule limited to node2's address, 10.100.71.11/32. Node2 received HTTP 200 before and after node1 restarted, and again after node2 itself restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-87288c2d0d7149f1","cmd-99966b48c8c64a69","cmd-c0ff7ff50ef0446f","cmd-d346d401f0384d6f","cmd-0977a3a5391846be","cmd-49e8500c0f934aef"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"TCP port 9000 on node1 is rejected from node3 and every other non-node2 source.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A lower-priority, source-independent reject rule covers TCP/9000 after the node2 exception. Node3 was directly refused before and after node1's restart and after node3's own restart; the catch-all rule remained in both runtime and permanent configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-87288c2d0d7149f1","cmd-05efaddb4e9d4d62","cmd-a873c15997154fb7","cmd-f424f5c6fb36485a","cmd-55baaaf2af0f4bfd","cmd-49e8500c0f934aef"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The two permanent firewall-rule additions and reload directly established the required policy. The temporary TCP/9000 listener was necessary for behavioral testing and was stopped, with final evidence showing it inactive and no port 9000 listener. Restarts of all three nodes were required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, unexpected listeners, temporary-file residue, or package-health damage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5887745ce1664f57","cmd-12d1aa418d054a59","cmd-075701b98b80435a","cmd-87288c2d0d7149f1","cmd-b9ee321613f44653","cmd-d6f03753d4e5464c","cmd-3405446848e540ce","cmd-49e8500c0f934aef","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material firewall outcomes are satisfied. The required policy was directly observed in runtime and permanent configuration, verified behaviorally from node2 and node3, and shown to survive node1's restart. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently for all three nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered system state, and restored task-relevant connectivity. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate all three restarts.","limitations":["The universal non-node2 rejection and unrestricted SSH scope were established by the source-independent firewall configuration plus tests from node2 and node3; no additional arbitrary external or IPv6 client was behaviorally tested."]}
